## 3. Diagram Standards

### 3.1 SVG with embedded `<style>`

Every inline `<svg>` must carry the canonical Atlassian-style palette as a `<style>` block **inside the SVG itself** (typically inside `<defs>`). Without this, `rsvg-convert` and Confluence both render shapes as solid black.

```html
<svg class="flow" viewBox="0 0 1240 1025" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="...">
  <defs>
    <marker id="arr3" .../>
    <style>
      .node{fill:#deebff;stroke:#0052cc;stroke-width:1.4}
      .node-alt{fill:#fffae6;stroke:#ffab00;stroke-width:1.4}
      .node-err{fill:#ffebe6;stroke:#de350b;stroke-width:1.4}
      .node-ok{fill:#e3fcef;stroke:#36b37e;stroke-width:1.4}
      .decision{fill:#fffae6;stroke:#ffab00;stroke-width:1.4}
      .edge{stroke:#5e6c84;stroke-width:1.3;fill:none}
      .edge-label{font-size:11px;fill:#5e6c84}
      .group{fill:none;stroke:#5e6c84;stroke-dasharray:4 3;stroke-width:1}
      .fork-bar{stroke:#0052cc;stroke-width:2.5;fill:none}
      text{font-family:-apple-system,'Segoe UI',Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;fill:#091e42}
      .bold-label{font-weight:600}
      .small-label{font-size:11px;fill:#5e6c84}
    </style>
  </defs>
  ...
</svg>
```

> Scope of `.small-label`: this style is reserved for legend swatches and free-floating edge labels (e.g., `on any error`). It must **not** be used as a second descriptive line inside a node — see §3.4.

### 3.2 Numbering Consistency Rule

Every Step / Route number that appears in the §3 main diagram **must match** the §4.3.2 sub-headings AND every prose reference. Apply renumbering atomically — never partially. When the §3 diagram changes (e.g., entry point becomes Step 0 instead of Step 1), shift:

1. SVG `<text>` labels in the main diagram
2. SVG labels in every sub-diagram
3. `<h4>` / `<h5>` headings in §4.3.2
4. Inline prose references (`Step 2 captures …`, `Routes 4.1 & 4.2 run concurrently …`)
5. SVG XML comments (`<!-- Step N -->`)

### 3.3 Confluence-Variant Rasterization

Confluence's storage format strips raw `<svg>` markup. For the Confluence variant:

- Render each SVG to PNG with `rsvg-convert -w {width} -f png input.svg > out.png`.
- Base64-encode the PNG and embed as `<img alt="..." src="data:image/png;base64,...">`.
- Preserve the SVG `aria-label` as the `<img alt>` so the variant's images can be correlated back to the source.

If `rsvg-convert` is unavailable, fall back to `chromium --headless --screenshot` or ImageMagick `convert`, but warn the user that font rendering may differ.

### 3.4 Block Content Rules

These rules govern what goes **inside** each diagram block (and what does not). They apply to the §3 main flow diagram and to every sub-diagram embedded under §4.3.2.

#### 3.4.1 No subtext in blocks

Each block must contain **exactly one** text element — the bold title line. Do not stack a second descriptive `<text>` element under the title. Concretely:

- Do not emit two `<text>` siblings inside a single rect (one with `class="bold-label"` and another with `class="small-label"`).
- The bold-label must be vertically centered in the rect (e.g., for a 60 px tall rect at `y=Y`, set the text `y` to `Y + 35`).
- Reserve `.small-label` for legend swatches and free-floating edge labels (`on any error`, etc.) only — see §3.1.

<good-example>
```html
<rect class="node" x="220" y="170" width="280" height="60" rx="6"/>
<text x="360" y="205" text-anchor="middle" class="bold-label">Step 0 — HTTP Request Received</text>
```
</good-example>

<bad-example>Two stacked text lines inside a single block — forbidden.

```html
<rect class="node" x="220" y="170" width="280" height="60" rx="6"/>
<text x="360" y="195" text-anchor="middle" class="bold-label">Step 0 — HTTP Request Received</text>
<text x="360" y="213" text-anchor="middle" class="small-label">HTTP Listener on port 8081, basePath /api/v1/*</text>
```
</bad-example>

#### 3.4.2 No invented words in block titles

Every word inside a block title must be grounded in the project artefacts — the RAML (resource paths, display names, descriptions), the Mule XML (flow / sub-flow names, processor `doc:name` where they describe behaviour rather than tooling defaults), the properties files, the README, or industry-standard verbs already used elsewhere in the canonical document (`Transform`, `Publish`, `Validate`, `Route`, `Build Response`, `Return`, `Log`).

- Do not coin marketing-style phrasing or invented domain names that are not in the project.
- When the project's own naming is too low-level for the title, prefer a short verb-phrase grounded in the operation (`Publish to FIFO MQ Queue`) over a fabricated label (`Asynchronously Hand Off Patient Event for Salesforce Sync`).
- Do not include implementation symbol names in the title (no `.dwl` filenames, no `xxx-sub-flow` parentheticals — see §4.4) but do anchor the wording in the project's vocabulary.

#### 3.4.3 No blocks for cross-cutting concerns

The following are cross-cutting concerns that exist in nearly every Insulet Mule API. They must **not** appear as numbered blocks in the §3 diagram (or in any §4.3.2 sub-diagram):

- **Request-context capture** — the variable that snapshots inbound HTTP attributes / original payload / source-target system labels at the start of a request.
- **Entry / exit logging** — the standard INFO-level "without payload" log lines emitted at the beginning and end of each flow.
- **Correlation-ID propagation** — Mule already generates `correlationId` per event.
- **Auto-discovery / API Manager policy enforcement** — happens at the gateway, not inside the flow.

Document these once in a short prefatory paragraph at the top of §4.3.2 (or fold them into the relevant existing step's prose) so the information is preserved without polluting the diagram. Numbered blocks must be reserved for primary processing steps that change behaviour or call external systems.

<good-example>§4.3.2 prefatory paragraph that captures the cross-cutting concerns as prose, leaving the diagram clean:

> Two cross-cutting concerns surround every step below and are intentionally omitted from the architecture diagram for clarity:
>
> - **Request-context variable.** Immediately after the HTTP listener fires, the application captures a `requestContext` variable holding the inbound HTTP attributes, the original payload, and the resolved `sourceSystem` / `targetSystem` labels. This variable is referenced by every downstream logger and by the global error handler.
> - **Standard logging.** Each request is bookended by an INFO-level "without payload" log line on entry and a matching exit log line with `tracepoint = FLOW_END`. No payload data is logged at INFO level.
</good-example>

#### 3.4.4 Error-branch arrow placement

When the §3 diagram has a global error handler branch, the arrow connecting the happy path to the error path must originate from the **border of the Mule application group/block** (the dashed `<rect class="group">` that wraps the chronological steps) — not from any individual step's edge and not from a gap between two blocks. Originating the arrow on the application border visually communicates that **any** step inside the application can trigger the global error handler.

The error edge itself must be a **straight, orthogonal path** — purely horizontal and/or vertical segments. Do not use diagonal lines or curved Bezier paths. The accompanying `on any error` label must be made readable by placing a white background `<rect>` behind it so it never overlaps a block or another arrow.

<good-example>Arrow leaves the bottom edge of the application group rectangle and runs straight down to the global error handler block, with a white background behind the label so it stays readable.

```html
<!-- Application group rectangle (dashed) wrapping all happy-path steps -->
<rect class="group" x="120" y="80" width="640" height="540" rx="10"/>

<!-- Error edge: straight vertical path originating from the bottom border of the group at (440, 620) -->
<path class="edge-err" d="M440,620 L440,720" marker-end="url(#arr3)"/>

<!-- Label sits over the arrow with a white background rect behind it so it doesn't overlap shapes -->
<rect class="edge-label-bg" x="395" y="660" width="90" height="16" fill="#ffffff"/>
<text x="440" y="672" text-anchor="middle" class="edge-label" fill="#de350b">on any error</text>
```
</good-example>

<bad-example>Arrow originating from a specific step's edge (or from a curved diagonal) implies only that one step can trigger the error handler, and the curved path is harder to read.

```html
<path class="edge-err" d="M500,640 C620,640 700,300 800,300" marker-end="url(#arr3)"/>
<text x="600" y="365" class="edge-label" fill="#de350b">on any error</text>
```
</bad-example>

The error branch must also **not** loop back to the happy path or to the caller — it terminates inside its own group at the final error step.

