#### 4.0.4 Rules for this table

- Emit **all 21 rows in the order shown** — do not reorder, do not drop rows even if the value is blank.
- Cell content is plain text or a hyperlink — no bullet lists, no nested tables, no images.
- Multi-value cells (e.g., dependent APIs, vault keys, runtime URLs) use `<br>` line breaks (HTML) or `<br/>` (Markdown HTML passthrough). Avoid commas-only when the list is longer than 3 items.
- Never embed credential values, encryption keys, or any secret material in the `Description` cell — only the **key/property name**.
- Hyperlinks (`<a href="...">`) are encouraged for GitHub, Exchange, API Manager, Monitoring, Runtime URLs.
- Do **not** add a `Notes`, `Owner`, `Last-updated` or any extra column — keep the table strictly two columns.

### 4.1 Input Tables (§4.3.1)

- Columns: `Field`, `Type`, `Required?`, `Description`.
- One row per field. **Never club** related fields into a single row.
- Do not add a `Notes` column.

### 4.2 Composite-Request Tables (§4.3.2)

For every API call (for e.g. Salesforce composite call), render a 4-column table:

| Field | Object | Type | Value Passed |
|-------|--------|------|--------------|

- `Field` — the Salesforce field API name (e.g., `FirstName`).
- `Object` — the Salesforce object (e.g., `Account`, `MemberPlan__c`).
- `Type` — Salesforce field type (`String`, `Date`, `Reference`, `Boolean`, …).
- `Value Passed` — the source expression. **Prefix with the inbound alias** when the value comes from the request payload (e.g., `ASPN.PatientFirstName` if the alias is `ASPN`). Use literal constants in quotes (`"Insulet"`). Use `vars.<name>` only for derived intermediates. Do not mention as-is names for variables/ sub-flows and dwl files.

If the user supplied a domain source doc (e.g., `ASPN Inbound System.docx`), embed the matching realistic JSON example **below** each composite-request table inside a `<pre><code>` block.

Do **not** include any column based on `referenceId` / refId mapping unless the user explicitly asks.

### 4.3 Property Substitution

Replace every `${prop.name}` token with its real, resolved value:

1. Look in `config.properties` first.
2. If absent, fall back to `config-dev.properties` and append the qualifier `(value in config-dev.properties)` after the substituted value. Replace the environment value with {env}. For e.g. dev-aspn-patient-sfl-mq -> {env}-aspn-patient-sfl-mq
3. If absent in both, leave the `${...}` token as-is and note it explicitly.

Example: `{env}-aspn-patient-sfl-mq`.

### 4.4 Forbidden Phrasings

- **Never name `.dwl` files** in prose, headings, captions, or tables. Describe behaviour, not the implementation symbol.
- **Never write `(sub-flow-name)` parentheticals** in headings (e.g., do not write "Step 4 — Account Upsert (account-cpa-objects-sub-flow)"). Drop the parenthetical.
- **No emojis** unless the user explicitly asks for them.

### 4.5 Code Blocks

- Use `<pre><code>...</code></pre>` for every JSON / SOQL / shell snippet.
- Indent JSON / SOQL bodies with **2 spaces** consistently.
- Do not let the Word exporter "rich-text-ify" code blocks; preserve them as plain `<pre>` so Word renders a mono-spaced run.

### 4.6 Tables

- Tables must be full-width: apply `style="width:100%"` on every `<table>` (or use a CSS rule that targets `table` in the document `<style>` block).
- Do not centre-align fixed-width tables.

### 4.7 Tone

- Developer-friendly, lucid, factual.
- Prefer flow diagrams over walls of prose.
- Avoid marketing language.
- Keep paragraphs short (≤ 4 sentences).

### 4.8 MUnit Section

The §5 MUnit Coverage section must be left **empty** unless the user provides explicit coverage figures or a Surefire report. Do not invent percentages.

### 4.9 Non-Functional Requirements Table (§2)

The §2 Non-Functional Requirements is a single, full-width, two-column table that captures the API's runtime SLA contract. Every Mule API document — regardless of layer (`exp` / `proc` / `sys`) — must use the same row schema, in the same order, so on-call engineers can compare SLAs across projects consistently.

#### 4.9.1 Column definition

| Column | Header | Description |
|--------|--------|-------------|
| 1 | `Type` | The NFR field name. Render verbatim from the schema below. |
| 2 | `Description` | The resolved value for this project. Use `TBC` only when the SLA is genuinely undecided — never invent. |

> Note: column header is `Type` / `Description` (not `Field` / `Value`) to match the existing Confluence Sample documents under the `SampleConfluenceDocuments` folder.

#### 4.9.2 Canonical row schema (preserve order, exactly 5 rows)

| # | Type | What to populate it with | Source |
|---|------|--------------------------|--------|
| 1 | Authentication | The wire-level auth mechanism enforced by API Manager / RAML, e.g., `Client ID Enforcement`, `JWT Validation (on /token endpoint), Client ID Enforcement (on all other endpoints)`, `Variable, see resources for review auth mechanism for each endpoint implementation`. | API Manager policies + RAML `securedBy` |
| 2 | Response Time | Percentile-based latency target, e.g., `95% within 60 seconds`, `99% within 2 seconds`. | NFR sheet / SRE runbook |
| 3 | Number of transactions to be processed | Expected throughput (per minute / per day) or `TBC` when not yet sized. | Capacity-planning doc |
| 4 | Spike Control | `Yes` / `No`. If `Yes`, append the policy name and threshold, e.g., `Yes — 100 req/sec burst`. | API Manager `spike-control` policy |
| 5 | Rate-Limiting SLA | `Yes` / `No`. If `Yes`, append the limit, e.g., `Yes — 1000 req/min`. | API Manager `rate-limiting` policy |

#### 4.9.3 Generic table template (drop into the canonical HTML)

Render in the canonical HTML as a single full-width table (apply the §4.6 width rule). The template below is the **exact structure** to emit; replace the `{{ ... }}` placeholders with resolved values, and use `TBC` (not blank) when an SLA is still being negotiated.

```html
<table style="width:100%">
  <thead>
    <tr><th style="width:30%">Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>{{ authentication }}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Response Time</td><td>{{ responseTime }}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Number of transactions to be processed</td><td>{{ throughput }}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Spike Control</td><td>{{ spikeControl }}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rate-Limiting SLA</td><td>{{ rateLimitingSla }}</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

Equivalent Markdown form (for the `.docx`/Markdown variant):

```markdown
| Type | Description |
|------|-------------|
| Authentication | {{ authentication }} |
| Response Time | {{ responseTime }} |
| Number of transactions to be processed | {{ throughput }} |
| Spike Control | {{ spikeControl }} |
| Rate-Limiting SLA | {{ rateLimitingSla }} |
```

#### 4.9.4 Rules for this table

- Emit **all 5 rows in the order shown** — do not reorder, do not drop rows even if the value is `TBC`.
- Cell content is plain text — no bullet lists, no nested tables, no images, no hyperlinks.
- `Spike Control` and `Rate-Limiting SLA` must be `Yes` or `No` (capitalised). When `Yes`, append the threshold after an em-dash, e.g., `Yes — 200 req/sec`.
- For Experience APIs that expose multiple auth mechanisms (e.g., JWT on `/token`, Client ID elsewhere), list each scope on its own line in the `Authentication` cell, separated by `<br>`.
- Do **not** add rows for Logging, Monitoring, Error-Handling, or Security beyond authentication — those belong in §1 (Application Overview) or in their own dedicated sections, not here.
- Do **not** add a `Notes`, `Owner`, `Reviewed-on`, or any extra column — keep the table strictly two columns.

