## 5. Output Format

### 5.1 Build the Canonical HTML First

Produce a single canonical HTML file with:
- A document-level `<style>` block at the top (table styling, code-block styling, headings, palette).
- Every diagram as **inline SVG** with the `<style>` block from §3.1 baked in.
- A table of contents linking to each section anchor (`<h1 id="s1">`, `<h2 id="s2">`, `<h2 id="s41">`, …).

### 5.2 Confluence Variant — `{project-name}-confluence.html`

Derived from the canonical HTML by:
1. Rasterising every SVG to a base64 PNG (§3.3) and replacing each `<svg>...</svg>` with `<img alt="..." src="data:image/png;base64,...">`.
2. Keeping all other markup intact.

This single file is what the user will import into Confluence.

### 5.3 Word Variant — `{project-name}.docx`

Derived from the canonical HTML by running it through Pandoc (preferred):

```bash
pandoc {project-name}.html -o {project-name}.docx \
  --reference-doc=reference.docx --highlight-style=tango
```

Constraints:
- Code blocks must remain `<pre>` elements pre-Pandoc so the resulting `.docx` shows them as a mono-spaced block (Word otherwise turns them into rich text and breaks indentation).
- Diagrams should already be rasterised PNG `<img>` tags before passing to Pandoc — Word does not handle inline SVG reliably.

If Pandoc is not available, use LibreOffice headless (`soffice --headless --convert-to docx`) or ask the user to install Pandoc.

### 5.4 File Naming and Location

Place both files in the user's chosen output folder:

```
<output-folder>/
├── {project-name}-confluence.html
└── {project-name}.docx
```

Example: `~/Documents/patient-proc-documentation/UpdatedDocumentation/patient-proc-api-confluence.html`.

